WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.820
CORS exists because browsers enforce the same-origin policy.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:11.960
The same-origin policy helps protect browser users by limiting what JavaScript loaded from one origin can read from another origin.

3
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:19.580
Without that browser rule, web pages would have a much broader ability to read responses from sites where the user may already have an active session.

4
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.700
An origin is defined by scheme, host, and port.

5
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:35.240
That means https on one host is a different origin from http on the same host, and the same host on a different port is also a different origin.

6
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:44.940
This detail matters in development and production because a frontend, API, documentation site, admin console, or local test client may all run on different origins.

7
00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:49.220
Modern applications often separate frontend and backend deployments.

8
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:55.720
A single-page application may run on one origin while an ASP.NET Core API runs on another.

9
00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:03.980
A partner portal, mobile web shell, or internal operations console may need selected browser-based access to API responses.

10
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:11.440
CORS creates controlled exceptions for those cases so approved browser clients can read selected responses when the server chooses to allow it.

11
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:16.520
A CORS decision should start from a business requirement, not from a browser error.

12
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:28.940
The useful questions are concrete: which frontend origin needs access, which API endpoint is involved, which methods are required, which request headers are needed, whether credentials are involved, and whether this behavior differs by environment.

13
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.680
If the team cannot answer those questions, the policy is not ready for production.

14
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.960
The defensive mindset is narrow and intentional.

15
00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:44.140
CORS does not make an API secure by itself, and it should not be treated as a general fix for connectivity problems.

16
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:56.080
It is one browser-facing control in a broader application design that still needs authentication, authorization, input validation, safe response shaping, logging, and operational review.
